<worksheet>
<title>Solving Quadratic Equations by Factoring</title>
<objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section we will see how to:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Apply the zero factor principle.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Factor a quadratic equation by factoring.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>
<p>
    When you multiply two (or more) things in an expression, the things are called factors. For example <m>(2x)(x-3)</m> consists of the factors <m>(2x)</m> and <m>(x-3)</m>.
    If you then set this expression equal to zero, you make an equation.
    <md>
    (2x)(x-3) = 0
    </md>
    If the product of two (or more) factors equals zero, then each of the factors could be zero. This is called the <term>zero product principle</term>.
</p>
<p>
    For quadratic equations equal to zero, we can factor it into linear terms and then set each linear term equal to zero. This turns solving one quadratic equation into
    solving two linear equations. It is not always possible to factor a quadratic equation, so we will see more techniques to solve quadratic equations in future sections.
</p>
<example>
    <title>Solve by Factoring</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Solve the quadratic equation <m>(x-3)(x+7)=0</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>x = 3, -7</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            This equation is already factored for us. (Yeah!) Set each factor to zero and solve. <m>x-3 = 0 \rightarrow x = 3</m> and <m>x+7 = 0\rightarrow x = -7</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<p>
    A quadratic equation where the left side is factored, is said to be in <term>factored form</term>.
</p>
<example>
    <title>Solve by Factoring</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Solve the quadratic equation <m>5(x+2)(2x-3)=0</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>x = -2, \frac{3}{2}</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            This equation is already factored for us. (Yeah!) The GCF of 5 can never be zero, so we can ignore it. Set the other factors to zero and solve.
            <m>x+2 = 0 \rightarrow x = -2</m> and <m>2x-3 = 0\rightarrow 2x = 3\rightarrow x = \frac{3}{2}</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<example>
    <title>Solve by Factoring</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Solve the quadratic equation <m>x^2+9x+20=0</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>x = -4,-5</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Start by factoring the left side of the equation. <m>x^2+9x+20 = (x+4)(x+5) = 0</m>. Now set each factor to zero and solve to get <m>x = -4 ,5</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<p>
<alert>Try it Now!</alert>
</p>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Solve the following quadratic equations by factoring.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>(x-6)(x-5)=0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>x^2+21x+108=0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>9x^2-42x+49=0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>8x^2+6x-9=0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>x^2=9x</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>x(x-7)=8</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>x^3-5x^2-4x+20=0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>
</worksheet>